.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SS "template<uint32_t dim, template< uint32_t > class STP>
.br
class csmp::ExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport< dim, STP >""generic_node_centered_finite_volumes\\ExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\&.h" 
.PP
\fBAuthor\fP
.RS 4
S\&.K\&. Matthaei 
.RE
.PP
\fBDate\fP
.RS 4
2002
.RE
.PP
.SH "Motivation"
.PP
The \fBExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP module is provided to allow users to perform passive advection and advection of a single non-wetting phase in two-phase flow simulations\&. It represents the fastest but CFL dependent transport scheme for arbitrary FE meshes including different FE types and mixes of volume and surface elements\&. 
.br

.PP
The scheme is also very tolerant in that no specific boundary conditions need to be applied\&. The value of the advected variable at inflow boundaries is not modified and governs what gets transported into the model domain\&. 
.br
.SH "Design Intent"
.PP
The scheme has been designed to capitalize on the new generic polytype CVFE technology that is part of CSMP\&. 
.br
.SH "Applicability"
.PP
Use the scheme if the transport velocity field is rapidly changing or invalidated by the transport itself\&. In this case the scheme is faster that the implicit scheme embodied in the FiniteVolumeTransport module which starts to pay off at about CFL=20 or greater\&. 
.br

.PP
Do not use this scheme when the CFL condition based transport increment leads global advection steps that are smaller than about 1/1,000 of the model length in the direction of the flow\&. In this case the computations would be prohibitively slow\&.
.SH "Structure"
.PP
The explicit advection scheme consists out of the three modules: 
.br

.PP
\fBExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP ExplicitFiniteVolumeAlgorithm ExplicitFiniteVolumeProcessor

.PP
The user will only interact with the \fBExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport\fP module, but the ExplicitFiniteVolumeAlgorithm is used to manage the accumulation and output of the solution in each transport step, and the ExplicitFiniteVolumeProcessor class facilitates the generation of the finite-volume stencils from each finite element, so that the accumulation can proceed on an element by element basis and no FV mesh needs to be stored\&.
.SH "Collaboration"
.PP
The scheme collaborates with the finite elements of the current model\&.
.SH "Consequences"
.PP
This implementation of the scheme cannot be restricted to Regions, but always needs to be applied to the entire model, i\&.e\&. \fBModel\fP\&. 
.br

.PP
Currently it also only works for 3D models\&. Surface computations can still be done if the mesh consists entirely of surface elements in 3D space\&.
.SH "Implementation"
.PP
The flux-balances are computed for all finite volumes including those which are located on the boundaries of the \fBRegion\fP or \fBModel\fP to which the transport scheme is applied\&. They are compensated for also on all domain boundaries\&. 
.br
.SH "Application Examples"
.PP
This is an example of how the second order accurate scheme is applied:

.PP
.PP
.nf
ExplicitNodeCenteredFiniteVolumeTransport  explicit_advector( model, "porosity", "diffusivity", 
                                                 "concentration", "velocity", second_order );

cout <<"\\n\\nadvectVariableExplicit: Configuring TRANSPORT simulation: ";
if ( second_order ) cout <<" IMPES: SECOND ORDER SCHEME\&."<< endl;
else                cout <<" IMPES: FIRST ORDER SCHEME\&."<< endl;
cout <<"\\nThe grid Courant number is "<< explicit_advector\&.CourantIncrement( sg ) << endl;
cout <<"\\nEnter advection time: ";
double time_interval;
cin >> time_interval;

cout <<"\\n\\tMeasuring the time required to solve the advection problem\&."<< endl;
clock_t ticks = clock();
explicit_advector\&.AdvectVariable( model, time_interval );
ticks = clock() \- ticks;
cout <<"\\n\\n\\tCPU clock ticks used for advection step: "<< ticks << endl;
.fi
.PP
 
